{:edb/db-before-t 2264 :edb/db-after-t 2265 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535577 50 #inst "2026-10-09T02:52:27.708Z" 13194139535577 true] [17592186355854 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355855 13194139535577 false] [17592186355854 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355889 13194139535577 true] [17592186355854 1100 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ for $-\\pi \\le x\\le 0$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" 13194139535577 false] [17592186355854 1100 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ for $-\\pi \\le x\\le 0$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$\n\nInterpret the printed inequalities literally. If they define a degenerate set, use the zero-area surface-integral convention. What is the flux?\n\nHint: First check whether $-x\\le y\\le0$ can have a nonempty interval when $x\\le0$. Treat the printed inequalities literally." 13194139535577 true] [17592186355854 1145 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)} \\\\  & =⟨{{1}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))-0\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+0)-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{-\\pi }^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-0+(-\\pi )-\\frac{(-\\pi )^{3}}{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535577 false] [17592186355854 1145 "The original inequalities are retained literally. Since $-\\pi\\le x\\le0$, we have $-x\\ge0$. The additional inequalities $-x\\le y\\le0$ are possible only when $-x=0$, so $x=0$ and $y=0$. Therefore the projection is $D=\\{(0,0)\\}$ and its two-dimensional area is zero. The graph $z=\\cos(x+y)$ over $D$ consists only of $(0,0,1)$; it is a degenerate surface, not a regular two-dimensional patch.\n\nUnder the zero-area surface-integral convention, both its surface area and its flux are zero:\n\n$$\\operatorname{area}(S)=0,\\qquad\\iint_S\\langle1,0,y\\rangle\\cdot d\\mathbf S=0.$$\n\nOne may extend the upward graph vector area element from the surrounding smooth graph: $(-g_x,-g_y,1)=(\\sin(x+y),\\sin(x+y),1)$. Its dot product with $F=\\langle1,0,y\\rangle$ is $\\sin(x+y)+y$. Integrating this bounded continuous function over the literal zero-area set $D$ yields zero.\n\nThe source accepted choice b, $\\pi-\\pi^3/6$, and its revealed solution instead evaluated the formal signed repeated integral\n\n$$\\int_{-\\pi}^{0}\\int_{-x}^{0}(\\sin(x+y)+y)\\,dy\\,dx=\\int_{-\\pi}^{0}\\left(1-\\cos x-\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right)\\,dx=\\pi-\\frac{\\pi^3}{6}.$$\n\nFor $x<0$, these inner limits are reversed. A formal signed integral over reversed limits does not integrate over the region described by the original inequalities. No corrected bounds are inferred. The study answer is $0$ for the original literal inequalities; the source's choice, grading and calculation remain preserved as evidence." 13194139535577 true] [17592186355889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355856 13194139535577 true] [17592186355889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355857 13194139535577 true] [17592186355889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355858 13194139535577 true] [17592186355889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355859 13194139535577 true] [17592186355889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355860 13194139535577 true] [17592186355889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355890 13194139535577 true] [17592186355889 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535577 true] [17592186355889 1041 "selection" 13194139535577 true] [17592186355889 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355890 13194139535577 true] [17592186355889 1110 #uuid "0b0d1580-cb70-50e0-aeb7-fd5dcae8bb21" 13194139535577 true] [17592186355890 1034 "0" 13194139535577 true] [17592186355890 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535577 true] [17592186355890 1111 #uuid "7cd88d8b-1154-5924-84b2-a8255f4e1264" 13194139535577 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-161481/literal-zero-area-v1/selection" 17592186355889 "q-161481/literal-zero-area-v1/selection/answer-zero" 17592186355890} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
